      "message": "These are dangerous and likely to cause \u0027Nexus5\u0027 failures, because allocators do not fail gracefully - the process terminates, instead of returning nullptr.\n\nThere are 3 work arounds\n1. detect advanced features such as dot product, I8MM etc, and infer its a new device that has more than 2GB of memory.\n2. wrap it in an #if !defined(DISABLE_SLOW_TESTS) so its not automatrically run on bots\nAlso this test and all the changes, are not really relevant on 32 bit.  int and ptrdiff are the same, and you cant have a valid memory block largers than 2GB.\nAlso they really are slow.  I made some passes at speeding up tests, really for the benefit of gemini running tests alot, and the \u0027Large\u0027 test is the single slowest test left.",
